  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 7,
    "lesson_title": "High-Pass Frequency Response",
    "lesson_core_move": "Convert the high-pass differential equation into the frequency response $H(j\\omega)$ that maps sinusoidal input frequency to output amplitude scaling and phase shift.",
    "section_count": 4,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Frequency Response",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to High-Pass Frequency Response",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Frequency response asks what the high-pass circuit does to one sinusoid at frequency $\\omega$. For that base case, we rewrite the differential equation in the frequency domain, where $X(j\\omega)$ and $Y(j\\omega)$ represent the input and output at the same sinusoidal frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "RC\\,\\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=y(t)+RC\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In steady state, a time derivative becomes multiplication by $j\\omega$ for a sinusoidal input. Apply that rule term by term to the high-pass equation: the derivative of the input becomes $j\\omega X(j\\omega)$, and the derivative of the output becomes $j\\omega Y(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "j\\omega RC\\,X(j\\omega)=(1+j\\omega RC)Y(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Once the derivatives have been replaced, the circuit equation is an algebraic relation between $X(j\\omega)$ and $Y(j\\omega)$. That is the form we need before we can isolate the response of the circuit itself.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "H(j\\omega)=\\frac{Y(j\\omega)}{X(j\\omega)}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This ratio is the frequency response. In the next steps, we will solve for $H(j\\omega)$ and use it to read the amplitude scaling and phase shift of the high-pass filter at each input frequency.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Frequency Response",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Replacing Derivatives with $j\\omega$ in the High-Pass Equation",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Convert the high-pass differential equation $RC\\,\\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=y(t)+RC\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}$ into its frequency-domain form by replacing each derivative with $j\\omega$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the high-pass differential equation from the previous step. For a single sinusoid at frequency $\\omega$, our goal is to rewrite that time-domain relation in terms of $X(j\\omega)$ and $Y(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "RC\\,\\frac{dx(t)}{dt}=y(t)+RC\\,\\frac{dy(t)}{dt}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "In steady state, each time derivative becomes multiplication by $j\\omega$. Apply that rule to both derivative terms, and keep the non-derivative terms as the frequency-domain quantities $X(j\\omega)$ and $Y(j\\omega)$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "j\\omega RC\\,X(j\\omega)=(1+j\\omega RC)Y(j\\omega)",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the algebraic frequency-domain relation for the high-pass circuit. We have not solved for $H(j\\omega)$ yet; this step only replaces the derivatives and puts the equation in the form we will use next.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Frequency Response",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Solving the High-Pass Equation for $H(j\\omega)$",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "Starting from $j\\omega RC\\,X(j\\omega)=(1+j\\omega RC)Y(j\\omega)$, solve for $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{Y(j\\omega)}{X(j\\omega)}$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the frequency-domain equation produced in the previous step. The ratio $Y(j\\omega)/X(j\\omega)$ is the transfer function, so isolate that ratio without moving on to interpretation yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nj\\omega RC\\,X(j\\omega) &= (1+j\\omega RC)Y(j\\omega) \\\\\n\\frac{Y(j\\omega)}{X(j\\omega)} &= \\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC} \\\\\nH(j\\omega) &= \\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That is the standard high-pass frequency response. The next step will use $H(j\\omega)$ to write the sinusoidal output in amplitude-phase form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 7,
        "lesson_title": "High-Pass Frequency Response",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Using $H(j\\omega)$ to Write the High-Pass Sinusoidal Output",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For an input sinusoid $x(t)=A\\cos(\\omega t+\\phi)$, use $H(j\\omega)=\\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC}$ to write the corresponding high-pass output in amplitude-phase form.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start with the input sinusoid and the transfer function at the same frequency $\\omega$. The output keeps the same angular frequency; only the amplitude and phase change.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\begin{aligned}\nH(j\\omega) &= \\frac{j\\omega RC}{1+j\\omega RC} \\\\\n|H(j\\omega)| &= \\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}} \\\\\n\\angle H(j\\omega) &= \\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\tan^{-1}(\\omega RC) \\\\\ny(t) &= A|H(j\\omega)|\\cos\\!\\bigl(\\omega t+\\phi+\\angle H(j\\omega)\\bigr) \\\\\n&= A\\frac{\\omega RC}{\\sqrt{1+(\\omega RC)^2}}\\cos\\!\\Bigl(\\omega t+\\phi+\\frac{\\pi}{2}-\\tan^{-1}(\\omega RC)\\Bigr)\n\\end{aligned}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The gain multiplies the input amplitude, and the phase of $H(j\\omega)$ adds to the input phase. That gives the real high-pass output in amplitude-phase form.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
